Two memory-safety bugs found via fuzzing: a heap-buffer-overflow in AVX-512 image rotation and unguarded memory access in Base64 decoding when assertions are stripped in release builds.

## Fixes

### Heap buffer overflow in `TransformImageRotate270<4>` (`SimdAvx512bwTransform.cpp`)

In the 4-row height residual loop, the inner column iterator over `width16` incremented by `8` instead of `16`, causing `TransformImageTranspose_4x4x16` (which processes 16 columns) to be called with overlapping indices — writing 64 bytes past the destination buffer end.

```cpp
// Before (wrong): processes 16 cols but advances only 8
for (; col < width16; col += 8)
    Avx512bw::TransformImageTranspose_4x4x16(...);

// After (correct)
for (; col < width16; col += 16)
    Avx512bw::TransformImageTranspose_4x4x16(...);
```

Triggered on a 32×4 BGRA32 image rotated 270°: `height4 == 4`, `height8 == 0`, `width16 == 32` — exactly the path where only the 4-row loop runs.

### Base64Decode crashes on invalid input in release builds (`SimdLib.cpp`)

All backends guard input validity with `assert(srcSize % 4 == 0 && srcSize >= 4)`, which is a no-op in release. Passing a string with length not a multiple of 4 (e.g., `"A"`) causes out-of-bounds reads. Added an explicit runtime check at the API dispatch level:

```cpp
if (srcSize < 4 || srcSize % 4 != 0)
{
    *dstSize = 0;
    return;
}
```

